  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nFriday night\n\nA northern stream shortwave trough will move east through the\nregion. The NBM has increased the precipitation chances across\nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley. However, the better \nmoisture and synoptic lift will be in southern Manitoba, \nsouthern Ontario, northern Minnesota, and northern Wisconsin. So\nthere continue to be questions on whether there will be any \nprecipitation in our area. \n\nAnother question is the freezing rain threat. Over the past 3 \nNBM model runs, the probabilities for measurable freezing rain \nin DESI has gradually decreased. The latest 13Z NBM has the \nmeasurable freezing rain north and northeast of our area. Due to\nthese trends, raised the hourly temperatures during the evening\nand early overnight. This lowered the probabilities of freezing\nrain north of Interstate 94 and there is now just patches of \nfreezing rain in this area. \n\nLow temperatures will be in the 20s.\n\nSaturday night and Sunday morning\n\nThe models are continuing to show some phasing between the \nnorthern and southern streams on Saturday night and Sunday\nmorning. While this is the case, there is concern that with the\nstronger system being in the southern stream that the \nprecipitation might be too far north. Over the past couple of\nmodel runs, the southern stream shortwave is strengthening and\ndigging further and further south. If this trend continues,\nthese precipitation chances will likely have to lowered or\nremoved. Wherever this precipitation ends up, boundary layer \ntemperatures might be cool enough on the northern edge for some \nlight snow.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-20s to lower 30s on\nSaturday night. High temperatures will be in the 30s.\n\nMonday through Thursday\n \nZonal flow will develop across the US/Canadian border for next\nweek. This will result in mainly above-normal temperatures.\nHigh temperatures will be in the 30s and 40s. Low temperatures\nwill be mainly in the 20s and lower 30s.",
